#{[17592186262714 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x=4(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4y=4(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(32\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}t)-(24\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =192t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =192(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =384\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262715 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve