$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262755 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262757 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262758 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/3a/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262759 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262760 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262762 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/24/24369439bc62cf06084a43d97dff0cee4e4089fd103511ef73d9dd5af1256ebb.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=30xy$ and $Q(x,y)=15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=30y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =30y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le y,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(30y+30y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}60y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y[x]_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y+\\sqrt{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+{y}^{3/2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =60(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262772 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+16{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)+9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+16}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262773 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{e}^{2t}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262794 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =|{e}^{t}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{2({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262798 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =3({t}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3({t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{3}+1+\\frac{1}{3}+1) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262801 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{2}+2)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t){|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+2)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+2+\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262810 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{2}+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t){|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}+2(0))-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+2(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262836 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(9\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,1,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262845 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [17592186262848 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k})-(6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-5)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-9\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262849 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}+12\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 3-3\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262863 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-12\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-12\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262864 "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262865 "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262873 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨6,1,7⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262874 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-9(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)+3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =0-27+15 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262878 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)-3\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t+1}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t+1}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{3(t+1)}\\right),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3(t+1)^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(0)-3\\mathbf{g}(0))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(0)-3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3(0)^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{0+1}⟩-3⟨-\\frac{1}{3(0+1)^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}0,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩-3⟨-\\frac{1}{3},1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩-⟨-1,3,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262883 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{q},$ $\\mathbf{r},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 9-4\\cdot 5)-2(3\\cdot 9-7\\cdot 5)-2(3\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-20+16-24 \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262884 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times (-7\\mathbf{c})) & =7 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-7)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =7 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262885 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨6,1,-9⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,1⟩ \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 5+(-9)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12+5-9 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262886 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨3,5,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6+5\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18-5-12 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262892 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))-2(4\\cdot 3-(-5)\\cdot (-1))+3(4\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12-14+30 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|28|=28.\n$$"] [17592186262893 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =14+12 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|26|=26.\n$$"] [17592186262894 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)-(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 3)+4(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-3k+1+32 \\\\  & =33-3k \\\\  & =3(11-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 3(11-k) & =0 \\\\ 11-k & =0 \\\\ k & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=11.$"] [17592186262897 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\sqrt{3-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3-t}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\pi t,\\hspace{0.167em}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(-1)-\\mathbf{g}(-1))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(-1)-{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(-1) \\\\  & =2⟨-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3-(-1)}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(-\\pi ),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{(-1)}⟩-⟨2(-1)-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\pi (-1),\\hspace{0.167em}-2(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨-2,-\\pi ,-1⟩-⟨-3,-2\\pi ,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2\\pi ,-2⟩-⟨-3,-2\\pi ,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262898 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & t & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t(2\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)-t(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 6)+(3\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-24t+18t+6 \\\\  & =-6t+6 \\\\  & =6(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 6(1-t) & =0 \\\\ 1-t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=1.$"] [17592186262899 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 7 \\\\ t & 4 & t \\\\ 2 & 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & t \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(4\\cdot 8-6\\cdot t)+(t\\cdot 8-2\\cdot t)+7(t\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =96-18t+8t-2t+42t-56 \\\\  & =30t+40 \\\\  & =10(3t+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 10(3t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186262900 "By the sum rule, we have \n \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{2}f(t)+5\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+5{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}(2\\sqrt{3+t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+t}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence , we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(1)+5\\mathbf{g}(1))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(1)+5{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(1) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(1-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(1) \\\\ 2(1+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262905 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ 0\