ed}\n$$"] [17592186262919 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y) &=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186262920 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f∇g+g∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}={y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =x{y}^{2}(-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2x{y}^{3}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262921 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =h(x,y,z)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xyz}({y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xy{z}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262923 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}2u),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{tan}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}u⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}(u(t)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u(t)},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(u(t))⟩\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}({t}^{2})⟩\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}({t}^{2})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262924 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2({e}^{t}) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262925 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & =2t⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩+2⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩+⟨2\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{ln}t+1,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262926 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{2}⟩+3{t}^{2}⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}⟩+⟨3{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}12{t}^{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262930 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t+1}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{2t}⟩+{e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{3t}⟩+⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{3t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}3{e}^{3t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265721 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $\