n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}x=0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186265852 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186265853 "First, we bring all variables to 